  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nNighttime microphysics satellite imagery continued to depict an\nimpressive band of persistent stratus, from the Texas Rio Grande\nup through West Texas, western Kansas, into Nebraska. There have\nbeen some temporary breaks in the stratus field across western\nzones over the past few hours, but the stratus is expected to\nprevail for one more day through much of Tuesday. South winds\nwill remain elevated through sunrise Tuesday, preventing fog \nformation through tonight. \n\nStrong south winds are expected Tuesday, in response to a mslp\ngradient of near 10 mb across the DDC CWA. 850 mb winds near \n2k ft AGL are progged to be near 45 mph after noon, after which\ntime gusts of that caliber can be expected. With such intense \nmixing, and 850 mb temperatures warming about 3C over Monday, \nstratus should break up at least at times or temporarily this \nafternoon. Still, stratus will prevail in this recirculating \npolar continental airmass Tuesday, and NBM is once again 2-3 \ndegrees too warm. After coordination with neighboring WFOs, \nlowered today's temperatures to the lower to mid 50s. \n\nShortwave will pass well north of SW KS Wednesday, and the\nresulting SWly downslope component to the wind fields will\nfinally push the stratus out of SW KS with sunshine returning.\nAfternoon temperatures well into the 60s will result. South flow\nwill maintain well above normal temperatures Thursday, once\nagain well into the 60s. \n\nModels remain consistent with previous solutions, showing a\npowerful trough entering the Great Basin 6 pm Thursday. In a\nmajor reversal though, all guidance is now much weaker, more\nprogressive, and farther north with the trough's ejection onto\nthe plains Friday. As such, rain chances have completely dried\nup with this system across Kansas, and NBM pops have correctly\nbeen removed (< 12%). Friday will now feature a warm and windy \nday, with above normal afternoon temperatures in the 60s, and \nsouth winds gusting near 45 mph ahead of the advancing \nassociated cold front. After the passage of the cold front\nFriday night, Saturday will be noticeably cooler with the\narrival of the trough axis, but only reduced to the 50s given\nthe incoming Pacific air mass. Sunrise temperatures Friday and\nSaturday will remain in the unseasonably mild 40s. Finally, a\nweak cool surface high pressure ridge behind the cold front may\nbe cold enough to support the first killing freeze of the \nseason Sunday morning, with NBM min temperature grids in the \nupper 20s. NBM currently is forecasting a low of 28 at DDC \nSunday morning, which would tie the record for the latest first \n28 degrees (November 22, 1934).",
